    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Alcohol use disorder, early recovery with one heavy-drinking day (F10.20)</content>. AUDIT-C has fallen from 10 to 5 and drinking days from most days to about four in six weeks. The one heavy-drinking day followed a run of nightmares early in exposure therapy, a common and expected strain point, and he used his relapse plan the next day. Blood pressure has fallen to 130/84 mmHg and weight by 1.3 kg. No withdrawal signs and no sign of liver disease on examination. [S]<list><item>Continue behavioral treatment and the veteran recovery peer group.</item><item>Naltrexone and acamprosate offered again; he prefers to continue without medication for now and will reconsider if heavy drinking recurs.</item><item>Relapse plan updated: on nights after a hard therapy session, call a peer and keep no alcohol in the house.</item><item>Hepatic panel and GGT to be drawn with the preventive-visit blood tests in the fall, as he prefers a single blood draw.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-traumatic stress disorder, improving in trauma-focused psychotherapy (F43.10)</content>. PCL-5 has fallen from 39 to 31, an 8-point fall, short of the 10 points usually taken as a meaningful change, but nightmares and startle are improving. Dr. Lee confirms the diagnosis and recommends finishing the prolonged exposure course. [S]<list><item>Continue weekly prolonged exposure with Dr. Lee.</item><item>No benzodiazepines or sedative sleep medicines.</item><item>Repeat PCL-5, AUDIT-C, and PHQ-9 at twelve weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Suicide risk and safety plan</content>. No suicidal ideation, intent, or plan since intake, and PHQ-9 item 9 is 0. Protective factors are unchanged: engagement in treatment, veteran and recovery peers, and a strong sense of service. Low acute risk. [S]<list><item>Safety plan reviewed and still current; lethal-means safety unchanged since intake.</item><item>Crisis line number confirmed in his phone: 988, press 1 for the Veterans Crisis Line.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Sleep disturbance with nightmares</content>. Improving with trauma treatment and less drinking; still about five and a half hours a night. [S]<list><item>Regular rise time, no alcohol as a sleep aid, and wind-down routine after therapy evenings.</item><item>If insomnia persists after the therapy course, offer cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>AUDIT-C</td><td>Baseline, then at six and twelve weeks by the care plan, and at every visit in early recovery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>5, down from 10; one heavy-drinking day since intake</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PCL-5</td><td>Baseline and during trauma-focused psychotherapy; care plan at six and twelve weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>31, down 8 points from 39</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>Baseline, then periodically while symptoms are followed</td><td>Completed today</td><td>6, down from 9; item 9 scored 0. No depressive disorder is diagnosed</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk reassessment and safety plan review</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No ideation, intent, or plan since the intake safety assessment; safety plan reviewed and current; lethal-means safety unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Trauma-focused psychotherapy (prolonged exposure)</td><td>Weekly course, usually 8 to 15 sessions</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Five sessions with Dr. Lee since early July; consultation report received today</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Recovery peer group and relapse plan</td><td>Weekly group; plan reviewed every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Attends most weeks; relapse plan updated for nights after therapy sessions</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Offer of medication for alcohol use disorder</td><td>At diagnosis and whenever drinking goals are not met</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Naltrexone and acamprosate re-offered after the heavy-drinking day; he prefers to continue behavioral treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hepatic panel and GGT</td><td>Baseline with heavy drinking, and before starting naltrexone</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Not drawn at intake despite near-daily heavy drinking, a gap in the baseline workup. No medication depends on it and there is no sign of liver disease today, so no harm is expected. Planned with his preventive-visit blood tests in the fall, his preference</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Avoid benzodiazepines and sedative-hypnotics</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None prescribed; no over-the-counter sleep aids</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure, weight, and signs of intoxication, withdrawal, or liver disease</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>130/84 mmHg, down from 134/86; weight 87.1 kg, down 1.3 kg; no withdrawal signs or liver stigmata</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sleep and nightmares</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>About five and a half hours a night; nightmares about twice a week, down from most nights</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Reduce AUDIT-C score from 10 to 3 or lower by twelve weeks. In progress; target 1957-09-30 [S]</item><item>Reduce PCL-5 score from 39 to 25 or lower by twelve weeks. In progress; target 1957-09-30 [S]</item><item>Attend at least eight integrated therapy or recovery-support sessions by twelve weeks. In progress; target 1957-09-30 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Substance Use Disorders (2021); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Management of Posttraumatic Stress Disorder and Acute Stress Disorder (2023); Recommend Evidence-Based Treatment: Know the Options (2026 accessed); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Weekly prolonged exposure with Dr. Lee and the recovery peer group. Three-month care-plan review with AUDIT-C, PCL-5, and PHQ-9. Call or use the crisis line at any time for thoughts of suicide or a return to heavy drinking. Next scheduled visit 1957-09-30: three-month recovery care-plan review. [S]</paragraph></text>

